<item>
  <title>Kitchener is answering the phone slower than it used to</title>
  <dc:creator>Greg Berberich</dc:creator>
  <link>https://chartingwaterlooregion.ca/posts/kitchener-phone-wait-times/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 





<p>Call the City of Kitchener on a weekday and the odds of someone picking up within half a minute have fallen from 80% in 2019 to 69% in 2023. The average wait in the queue went from 26 seconds to 58 seconds. Call volumes barely moved over the same period, so the slowdown is not a story about more people phoning in.</p>
<section id="the-80-target-held-until-2021-then-slipped" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="the-80-target-held-until-2021-then-slipped">The 80% target held until 2021, then slipped</h2>
<p>The City measures itself against a common contact-centre yardstick: answer 80% of calls within 30 seconds. On weekdays in 2019 it met that target almost exactly. Through the first two pandemic years it stayed close, with the best months of all in the quiet January and February of 2020 and 2021, when more than 90% of calls were answered on time.</p>
<p>The break came in 2022. Every month that year was below target, and June 2020 was the low point, with 60% of calls answered within 30 seconds. The first seven months of 2023 look much like 2022.</p>
<p><picture> <source media="(max-width: 767px)" srcset="figures/fig-answered-phone.png" width="403" height="432"> <img src="https://chartingwaterlooregion.ca/posts/kitchener-phone-wait-times/figures/fig-answered.png" alt="Line chart of the monthly share of calls answered within 30 seconds. The line sits near the 80% target through 2019, 2020 and 2021 with a dip in mid-2020, then drops below the target for all of 2022 and 2023, reaching a low near 60% in May 2022." class="img-fluid" width="797" height="480"> </picture></p>
</section>
<section id="the-average-wait-more-than-doubled" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="the-average-wait-more-than-doubled">The average wait more than doubled</h2>
<p>Averages hide a lot in a phone queue, since most calls are answered quickly and a few wait a very long time. Even so, the direction is clear. A weekday caller in 2019 waited 26 seconds on average. In 2022 that was 63 seconds, and the first seven months of 2023 averaged 58 seconds.</p>
<p>Volume does not explain it. The centre handled 329 calls on a typical weekday in 2019 and 351 in 2023, a rise of 7%. The data says nothing about staffing, which is the obvious place to look next.</p>
<p><picture> <source media="(max-width: 767px)" srcset="figures/fig-wait-phone.png" width="403" height="365"> <img src="https://chartingwaterlooregion.ca/posts/kitchener-phone-wait-times/figures/fig-wait.png" alt="Bar chart of average queue time by year: about 26 seconds in 2019, 29 in 2020, 37 in 2021, 63 in 2022 and 58 in the first seven months of 2023." class="img-fluid" width="797" height="384"> </picture></p>
</section>
<section id="early-summer-is-the-crunch" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="early-summer-is-the-crunch">Early summer is the crunch</h2>
<p>Whatever the year, the phones are busiest in June and quietest in December, and the answer rate follows the volume: over the whole period, 69% of June calls were answered on time against 81% in January. Property tax bills, summer recreation registration and moving season all land in the same few weeks. A centre that just meets its target in a quiet month will miss it in a busy one unless staffing moves with the calendar.</p>
<p><picture> <source media="(max-width: 767px)" srcset="figures/fig-season-phone.png" width="403" height="384"> <img src="https://chartingwaterlooregion.ca/posts/kitchener-phone-wait-times/figures/fig-season.png" alt="Bar chart of average weekday calls by month of the year. Calls climb from under 300 a day in December and January to a peak near 400 in June, then fall through the autumn. June and July are highlighted." class="img-fluid" width="797" height="384"> </picture></p>
</section>
<section id="data-and-methods" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="data-and-methods">Data and methods</h2>
<p>The City of Kitchener publishes daily metrics for its main phone queue on its open data portal. This post uses the export covering 26 February 2019 to 30 July 2023, the full range available when it was downloaded in February 2025. Days with an incomplete or duplicated set of metrics (14 of about 1,600) and the partial first month were dropped.</p>
<p>All figures are weekdays excluding Ontario statutory holidays. Weekend and holiday volumes run at roughly 31% of a weekday, on a reduced service, and would flatter every average.</p>
<p>“Answered within 30 seconds” is the share of all calls presented that an agent picked up inside the target, so a caller who hung up while waiting counts as a miss. The City also reports three gentler versions of the same measure, all included in the download below. Waits are the daily average queue time weighted by the number of calls that day.</p>
